`, <url>...</url> for URLs, <a href=...> for hyperlinks, and <par/> for paragraph breaks.\nL48: \nL49: Verification against PDF Ensure that the new title/authors match the snapshot below. (If there is no snapshot or it is too small, consult cite0†the PDF .)\nL50: Authors concatenated from the text boxes above:\nL51: \nL52: [Input] ALL author names match the snapshot above—including middle initials, hyphens, and accents.\nL53: \nL54: [Button: Create GitHub issue for staff review]\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: ##### Abstract\nL58: Although measuring held-out accuracy has been the primary approach to evaluate generalization, it often overestimates the performance of NLP models, while alternative approaches for evaluating models either focus on individual tasks or on specific behaviors. Inspired by principles of behavioral testing in software engineering, we introduce CheckList, a task-agnostic methodology for testing NLP models.\nL59: CheckList includes a matrix of general linguistic capabilities and test types that facilitate comprehensive test ideation, as well as a software tool to generate a large and diverse number of test cases quickly. We illustrate the utility of CheckList with tests for three tasks, identifying critical failures in both commercial and state-of-art models. In a user study, a team responsible for a commercial sentiment analysis model found new and actionable bugs in an extensively tested model.\nL60: In another user study, NLP practitioners with CheckList created twice as many tests, and found almost three times as many bugs as users without it.\nL61: Anthology ID:\nL62:     2020.acl-main.442\nL63: Volume:\nL64:     cite28†Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics L65: Month:\nL66:     July\nL67: Year:\nL68:     2020\nL69: Address:\nL70:     Online\nL71: Editors:\nL72:     cite29†Dan Jurafsky , cite30†Joyce Chai , cite31†Natalie Schluter , cite32†Joel Tetreault L73: Venue:\nL74:     cite33†ACL L75: SIG:\nL76: \nL77: Publisher:\nL78:     Association for Computational Linguistics\nL79: Note:\nL80: \nL81: Pages:\nL82:     4902–4912\nL83: Language:\nL84: \nL85: URL:\nL86:     cite34†https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.442/ L87: DOI:\nL88:     cite35†10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.442†doi.org L89: Award:\nL90:  Best Overall Paper\nL91: Bibkey:\nL92: \nL93: Cite (ACL):\nL94:     Marco Tulio Ribeiro, Tongshuang Wu, Carlos Guestrin, and Sameer Singh. 2020. cite34†Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList . In Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, pages 4902–4912, Online. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL95: Cite (Informal):\nL96:     cite34†Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList (Ribeiro et al., ACL 2020)\nL97: Copy Citation:\nL98:     [Button: More options…]\nL99: PDF:\nL100:     cite23†https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.442.pdf L101: Video:\nL102:     cite36† http://slideslive.com/38929272†slideslive.com L103: cite23†PDF cite0†Cite cite36†Video†slideslive.com cite0†Fix data L104: \nL105: * * *\nL106: ##### Export citation\nL107: \nL108:     @inproceedings{ribeiro-etal-2020-beyond,\nL109:         title = \"Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of {NLP} Models with {C}heck{L}ist\",\nL110:         author = \"Ribeiro, Marco Tulio  and\nL111:           Wu, Tongshuang  and\nL112:           Guestrin, Carlos  and\nL113:           Singh, Sameer\",\nL114:         editor = \"Jurafsky, Dan  and\nL115:           Chai, Joyce  and\nL116:           Schluter, Natalie  and\nL117:           Tetreault, Joel\",\nL118:         booktitle = \"Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nL119:         month = jul,\nL120:         year = \"2020\",\nL121:         address = \"Online\",\nL122:         publisher = \"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nL123:         url = \"https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.442/\",\nL124:         doi = \"10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.442\",\nL125:         pages = \"4902--4912\",\nL126:         abstract = \"Although measuring held-out accuracy has been the primary approach to evaluate generalization, it often overestimates the performance of NLP models, while alternative approaches for evaluating models either focus on individual tasks or on specific behaviors. Inspired by principles of behavioral testing in software engineering, we introduce CheckList, a task-agnostic methodology for testing NLP models.\nL127: CheckList includes a matrix of general linguistic capabilities and test types that facilitate comprehensive test ideation, as well as a software tool to generate a large and diverse number of test cases quickly. We illustrate the utility of CheckList with tests for three tasks, identifying critical failures in both commercial and state-of-art models. In a user study, a team responsible for a commercial sentiment analysis model found new and actionable bugs in an extensively tested model.\nL128: In another user study, NLP practitioners with CheckList created twice as many tests, and found almost three times as many bugs as users without it.\"\nL129:     }\nL130: Download as File\nL131: \nL132:     <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\nL133:     <modsCollection xmlns=\"http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3\">\nL134:     <mods ID=\"ribeiro-etal-2020-beyond\">\nL135:         <titleInfo>\nL136:             <title>Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList</title>\nL137:         </titleInfo>\nL138:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL139:             <namePart type=\"given\">Marco</namePart>\nL140:             <namePart type=\"given\">Tulio</namePart>\nL141:             <namePart type=\"family\">Ribeiro</namePart>\nL142:             <role>\nL143:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL144:             </role>\nL145:         </name>\nL146:         <name